WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:06.000
Westworld Season 2 Episode 3 begins in a new
park called the Raj.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.910
Just like Westworld is based on the American
Wild West, and Shogun World on feudal Japan,

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.920
the Raj is based on the Raj period in India,
when it was ruled by the British Empire . So

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.920
we see hookahs and rickshaws and peacocks
and elephants, and someone plays a sitar,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.050
but the song is Seven Nation Army – cause
why let historical accuracy get in the way

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.390
of a banger?

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:30.970
The guests Grace and Nicholas hook up, but
Grace suspects that Nic might be a host . And

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.570
she doesn’t want to fuck a robot, because
they’re not free, they “just do as [they’re]

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:36.570
told”.

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.050
But Nic says doing what you’re told can
be “half the fun” – he likes being controlled.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:46.060
So even this random sex scene raises questions
about freedom and control – which is what

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.250
this show’s all about.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:52.989
To make sure Nic is human, Grace shoots him,
because the high-tech bullets in Westworld

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:58.080
are coded to only hurt hosts . At least, they
were, until Dolores’ rebellion, when Ford

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.510
reprograms the guns to kill humans as well
. Which means these Raj scenes are a flashback

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.150
happening sometime in Season 1.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.990
Grace and Nic go on a tiger hunt, but Grace
is hunting for something else – she has

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:16.860
a map with this hexagonal pattern which also
appears around Delos’ secret data project.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.530
So is Grace a Delos employee, helping them
secure their data?

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.440
Or does she have her own plans?

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.860
The hosts in the Raj go rogue and start killing
guests.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.100
So this bit probably happens round the same
time that the Westworld hosts rebel.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.190
Grace escapes, then gets chased by a tiger
– the same animal they’d been hunting.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.619
Across the parks, the humans have become the
hunted.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.750
The tiger knocks Grace off a cliff, and they
wash up in Westworld.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:47.869
The tiger dies and becomes the same tiger
we see in Episode 1.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:53.229
Then Grace meets some hosts of Ghost Nation
– who have secrets of their own.

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:58.050
We jump to the two weeks ahead timeline, with
Bernard, Strand’s security, and Charlotte.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:02.990
Maling mentions that “Someone took out the
Cradle” . According to a Westworld email,

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:08.940
the Cradle is a “simulation technology”
that stores and tests park storylines . Online,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:14.620
the Cradle appears as a red U-shaped room,
which looks like this room in a trailer.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.659
The Cradle might connect to Delos’ secret
project of recording guest behaviour . Because

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:24.169
that behaviour data might help with simulating
behaviour in the Cradle.

00:02:24.169 --> 00:02:28.900
And maybe, if you could perfectly simulate
people, you could make copies of people as

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.549
robots, like in the Futureworld movie.

00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:36.749
This could explain this shot in an alternate
trailer, which seems to show old William with

00:02:36.749 --> 00:02:38.329
James Delos.

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:43.689
James was sick and dying thirty years ago
. So how is he alive in the present?

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:49.920
Maybe this James is a robot clone of the original,
made with data in the Cradle.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.239
But then why would someone ‘take out’
the Cradle?

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:57.280
Maybe for the same reason someone flooded
this Valley, where the data project seems

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.280
to be.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.590
William described this place as his greatest
mistake – maybe he destroys it.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.809
Bernard flashes back a week to the present
timeline, where he and Charlotte search for

00:03:07.809 --> 00:03:12.370
Peter Abernathy – because Peter has secret
Delos data in his head.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:17.639
They find Peter with the host Rebus, who’s
selling people as slaves to the Confederados.

00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:22.120
We don’t know if Rebus is programmed to
do this, or if he’s choosing to do it cause

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.419
he’s just immoral.

00:03:23.419 --> 00:03:29.540
But then, Bernard reprograms Rebus’ personality,
to make him a virtuous good guy . Which explains

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:35.180
why, a week later in Episode 1, Rebus tries
to protect another host . When the Confederados

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.670
arrive, Rebus fights to protect his former
slaves – is Rebus acting freely here?

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:45.859
He says he’s “heedin' [his] convictions”
, but Bernard just changed Rebus’ convictions.

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:50.949
This could illustrate the idea that even if
we are free, we’re bound to follow our personalities

00:03:50.949 --> 00:03:52.249
and values.

00:03:52.249 --> 00:03:56.549
In the chaos, Charlotte escapes, but Bernard
and Peter are captured.

00:03:56.549 --> 00:04:01.749
Dolores takes over the Confederado army, promising
to help them beat the humans , and get to

00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:06.069
Glory – which is the flooded valley weapon
data place.

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:10.489
Dolores has Clementine, who’s acting like
a murder-zombie since she got lobotomised

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:11.739
last season.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:14.989
Clementine was built to be killed without
mercy.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:17.310
Now she kills without mercy.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.960
Peter arrives, and has an emotional reunion
with Dolores, because he was her host father

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.110
back at their ranch.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.950
But Peter’s brain is scrambled ever since
Charlotte and Lee filled it with Delos data

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:33.410
last season . Charlotte tried to smuggle Peter
out of the park on a train – which is why

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:39.320
he keeps saying “I need to get to the train”
. His mind keeps bouncing between old personalities

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:44.190
. So sometimes he speaks like Dolores’ father
. But sometimes he speaks like “the professor”,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:50.590
an old role he played who liked to quote Shakespeare
. So Pete recites lines from King Lear . Dolores

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.910
feels conflicted.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:56.730
Cause she wants to reject her old fake life
and wage her war against humans.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.980
She tells the Confederados to call her Wyatt
– the bad guy role.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:07.050
But Dolores still loves the men she was programmed
to love – Peter and Teddy . So kind of like

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:10.130
Peter, Dolores is torn between different identities.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.590
Maybe that means she’s a malfunctioning
machine.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:19.650
Or, maybe, being truly conscious and human
means juggling different identities and roles.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:25.220
While Dolores shows love for Peter, she’s
harsh to Bernard – tossing him in jail . Maybe

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.610
she’s mad that he was her jailer in Westworld
for all those years.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:33.550
But at the same time, she recognises Bernard
as the replacement of Arnold – Dolores’

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.650
creator, who she saw as a sort of a father.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:41.640
So she wants Bernard to approve of her plan
to “dominate” the “world” . But Bernard’s

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.960
not so sure about her war.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:49.350
Bernie looks at the data in Peter’s head,
and is shocked, though we only see this hexagonal

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.380
symbol from the Delos data project and Grace’s
notebook.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:58.530
Dolores fights a battle against Delos security,
who decide their best tactical option is to

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.490
slowly walk across a field towards the death-bots.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:07.860
Dolores blows up the humans, but she also
betrays and kills the Confederados . Why does

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.110
Dolores kill other hosts?

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:14.720
She tells them the same thing she tells a
Ghost Nation host in Ep 1 – that “we don't

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.120
all deserve to make it” to the “the valley
beyond” . Dolores judges the Confederados

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.310
unworthy.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:25.050
And they do seem like pricks , but they were
programmed that way, right?

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:29.050
We just saw how Rebus can be switched from
a villain to a hero – so can you really

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.200
judge a machine for following its code?

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.670
Maybe Dolores blames the hosts for not awakening
to full consciousness like she did.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:40.600
But it took Dolores decades of wandering the
maze to awake.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.070
So is it really fair to blame these guys?

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:49.200
Dolores tells Teddy to execute the Confederado
survivors, but he disobeys and lets them go.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.639
Teddy is finally thinking for himself.

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:53.750
But how will Dolores react?

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:58.620
Also, during the battle Charlotte takes Peter
– but we know from the week ahead timeline

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.960
that she later loses him again.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.000
And Charlotte seem to suspect that Bernard
is responsible .

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.170
Maeve Hector and Lee are on their way to find
Maeve’s daughter, when they encounter some

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:11.190
hosts of Ghost Nation.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:15.730
Maeve recognises one host who attacked her
in her past life at the homestead.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:20.450
The hosts demand that Maeve give them Lee
. Maeve refuses, and tries to use her power

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.720
to control them, but they resist her command.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.650
So Maeve and co run away.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.920
Why are these hosts immune to Maeve’s Jedi
mind tricks?

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.200
She’s commanded Bernard, a sheriff, Lee’s
cannibal.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.630
What makes the Ghost Nation hosts different?

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.670
And why do they want Lee, but not Maeve or
Hector?

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.480
Is it because Lee is human?

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:43.310
They did capture Stubbs last season, and now
apparently Grace.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:47.330
It looks like Ghost Nation is collecting humans,
but why?

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:50.700
Some fans theorise that they’re trying to
protect humans, possibly they’re working

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:51.960
with Elsie.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.550
One way or another, Ghost Nation will be a
big part of this season, especially the character

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.070
Akecheta, who also appears in Maeve’s flashback.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.710
Sizemore gets mad about Maeve and Hector’s
relationship.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:07.960
He wrote these characters, and he’s upset
to see them change from his script, like an

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.420
author who doesn’t like fanfiction.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:15.510
Hector says that his old scripted love was
fake, but his new love with Maeve is real

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:21.100
. But then Lee shows that Hector uses scripted
dialogue to describe his new love . So does

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.790
this mean Hector’s love isn’t real?

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:28.430
Or is he just using the words he knows to
express something true?

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:34.050
Maybe all of us are limited by our language,
and culture – it’s our programming . Maeve

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:37.449
meets up with Armistice, Felix and Sylvester.

00:08:37.449 --> 00:08:41.279
Armistice has a new arm, because she lost
her old one last season.

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:46.690
The party passes through a snowy area, which
might suggest that Delos can control the weather?

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:48.720
And they encounter a warrior from Shogunworld.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.730
We don’t know if this guy came across to
Westworld from Shogunworld, or if Lee’s

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:58.080
directions are so bad that they wandered into
Shogunworld.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.610
Or maybe Lee deliberately misled them.

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.319
However it happened, this culture clash may
complicate Maeve’s quest.

00:09:05.319 --> 00:09:09.850
So this episode focusses closely on the central
questions of Westworld.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:15.740
Like, what is freedom, and how do our desires
and personalities and identities affect that?

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.149
Can you morally judge a machine?

00:09:18.149 --> 00:09:20.139
What does love mean to a machine?

00:09:20.139 --> 00:09:25.839
And what kinda Black Mirror Bladerunner shenanigans
can we achieve with big data and robotics?

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:29.410
Westworld is trying to ask some really big
questions.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:35.089
For more Westworld analysis, check out the
subreddit, and the podcasts Decoding Westworld,

00:09:35.089 --> 00:09:36.899
Bald Move and others.

